'''''Dischidia nummularia''''' is an [[Epiphyte|epiphytic]] climbing plant that belongs to the genus ''[[Dischidia]]''.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.llifle.com/Encyclopedia/SUCCULENTS/Family/Asclepiadaceae/32546/Dischidia_nummularia|title=Dischidia nummularia R.Br. Prodr. Fl. Nov. Holland. 461 1810 : Family: ASCLEPIADACEAE|website=Llifle.com|accessdate=2017-07-20}}</ref> It has tiny, opposite lens-shaped leaves, and is frequently seen on the trunks of trees. In the wild it is found in [[India]], [[China]], [[Indonesia]], [[Laos]], [[Thailand]], [[Vietnam]], [[Myanmar]], [[Malaysia]], and [[Australia]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=World Checklist of Selected Plant Families: Royal Botanic Gardens, Kew|url=https://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=504655|access-date=2021-07-08|website=wcsp.science.kew.org}}</ref> It is commonly referred to as '''string-of-nickels''' or '''button orchid'''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.gardenia.net/plant/dischidia-nummularia-string-nickels-grow-care-guide|title=Dischidia nummularia (String of Pearls)|website=Gardenia.net|accessdate=2024-05-13}}</ref>[[File:Dischidia nummularia.jpg|thumb|''Dischidia nummularia'' flowers]]

